      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">1. Understanding the SOC 2 Type 2 Continuous Observation Window</h2>
        <p>
          Unlike a SOC 2 Type 1 examination—which audits whether controls are suitably designed at a single point in time (e.g. at 11:59 PM on June 30)—a <strong>SOC 2 Type 2 examination</strong> evaluates the <em>operational effectiveness</em> of controls throughout a continuous historical window.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under the AICPA's Trust Services Criteria (TSP Section 100), the licensed independent CPA firm must inspect random sample populations across the entire duration. If an automated control (such as CloudTrail logging or S3 bucket encryption) fails silently for three weeks in the middle of a 6-month window, the auditor must note a control deficiency or issue a qualified report.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is where compliance automation platforms provide existential leverage: they run automated API tests every 24 hours, notifying engineering teams immediately when a control drifts out of compliance.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
