      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">3. Workstation Agent Deployment: OSQuery vs MDM Direct Ingestion</h2>
        <p>
          Verifying security controls on employee laptops (Trust Services Criterion CC6.1 and CC6.6) represents a persistent friction point for remote engineering teams. The required checks include:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>Full Disk Encryption:</strong> FileVault 2 (macOS) or BitLocker (Windows) active and operating.</li>
          <li><strong>Screen Lock Timeout:</strong> Enforced auto-lock after maximum 10 to 15 minutes of inactivity.</li>
          <li><strong>Auto-Updates Enabled:</strong> Operating system updates installed within 30 days of release.</li>
          <li><strong>Password Manager & EDR:</strong> Verified presence of enterprise password managers (1Password, Bitwarden) and endpoint detection (SentinelOne, CrowdStrike).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          While both platforms offer desktop agents, mature organizations bypass desktop agents entirely by connecting Drata or Vanta directly to their <strong>Mobile Device Management (MDM)</strong> suite (such as Kandji, Jamf Pro, or Microsoft Intune). The MDM reports hardware state via webhooks, eliminating the need for employees to install third-party background software.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">4. Managing Audit Window Control Drift: The 72-Hour Remediation SLA</h2>
        <p>
          In a 6-month observation window (180 days), automated test failures will inevitably occur. A developer might temporarily generate a public S3 bucket for frontend asset testing, or an offboarded contractor's GitHub access might linger for 48 hours.
        </p>
        <p>
          The difference between a clean SOC 2 Type 2 report and a qualified opinion hinges on your team's <strong>Remediation SLA</strong>:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>Automated Alerting:</strong> Connect Drata/Vanta test failures directly to a dedicated PagerDuty or Slack channel (<code>#soc2-alerts</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>72-Hour Cure Window:</strong> If a test fails and is resolved within 72 hours with an attached Jira ticket explaining root cause and resolution, auditors classify the incident as an isolated anomaly.</li>
          <li><strong>Documenting Auditor Notes:</strong> In the platform dashboard, enter an explanation in the "Auditor Notes" field referencing the pull request or commit that restored compliance.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">5. Automated Pre-Audit AWS IAM & Infrastructure Diagnostic Script</h2>
        <p>
          Run this automated Python verification script using Boto3 to audit high-severity SOC 2 CC6 infrastructure controls before opening your observation window:
        </p>

<pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono overflow-x-auto text-cyan-400">
import boto3
from typing import List, Dict, Any

def audit_soc2_aws_controls() -> Dict[str, Any]:
    iam = boto3.client('iam')
    s3 = boto3.client('s3')
    
    findings = []
    
    # Control CC6.1: Verify root account has zero active access keys
    root_summary = iam.get_account_summary()
    if root_summary['SummaryMap'].get('AccountAccessKeysPresent', 0) > 0:
        findings.append({
            "control": "CC6.1 - Root Key Elimination",
            "status": "FAIL",
            "severity": "CRITICAL",
            "message": "AWS Root account has active access keys! Delete immediately."
        })
    else:
        findings.append({
            "control": "CC6.1 - Root Key Elimination",
            "status": "PASS",
            "severity": "INFO",
            "message": "Root account access keys are not present."
        })
        
    # Control CC6.6: Verify S3 Block Public Access is active at account level
    try:
        s3control = boto3.client('s3control')
        caller_id = boto3.client('sts').get_caller_identity()['Account']
        pab = s3control.get_public_access_block(AccountId=caller_id)
        config = pab['PublicAccessBlockConfiguration']
        
        all_blocked = all([
            config.get('BlockPublicAcls'),
            config.get('IgnorePublicAcls'),
            config.get('BlockPublicPolicy'),
            config.get('RestrictPublicBuckets')
        ])
        
        findings.append({
            "control": "CC6.6 - S3 Account Public Access Block",
            "status": "PASS" if all_blocked else "FAIL",
            "severity": "HIGH",
            "message": "All 4 S3 public block toggles enabled." if all_blocked else "Account-level S3 public access is not fully blocked."
        })
    except Exception as e:
        findings.append({
            "control": "CC6.6 - S3 Account Public Access Block",
            "status": "FAIL",
            "severity": "HIGH",
            "message": f"Could not verify S3 Public Access Block: {str(e)}"
        })
        
    return {
        "audit_ready": all(f["status"] == "PASS" for f in findings),
        "total_controls_tested": len(findings),
        "findings": findings
    }

if __name__ == "__main__":
    report = audit_soc2_aws_controls()
    print(f"Pre-Flight Compliance Status: {'PASSED' if report['audit_ready'] else 'ACTION REQUIRED'}")
    for item in report["findings"]:
        print(f"[{item['status']}] {item['control']}: {item['message']}")
</pre>
